From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Darren Shepherd <darren@obot.ai>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 08:17:26 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix(control): ghostty's own ctrl+enter keybinding ate the key
 (KD-30)

Reported from use: ctrl+enter did nothing in a ghostty pane while it worked
in a cascadia one. Measured with a byte dumper across all three keyboard
modes, and nothing arrived in any of them - which is what pointed at a
binding rather than at an encoding, since an encoding cannot be wrong in
legacy, modifyOtherKeys and kitty mode identically.

ghostty ships a full application's worth of keybindings and they are still
live inside a WT pane. Two of them are on Enter:

    ctrl+enter       -> toggle_fullscreen
    ctrl+shift+enter -> toggle_split_zoom

`ctrlOrSuper` is super on macOS and ctrl everywhere else, so on Windows
those are exactly the chords reported. Surface.maybeHandleBinding runs
before any encoding, so the encoder was never reached.

Declining the action does not hand the key back, and that is the part worth
remembering. GhosttyControlCore reports both unhandled - WT owns window
chrome and pane zoom - and DESIGN.md says returning false for the tab and
split actions beside them is "correct, not a gap". It is correct and it is
not sufficient: a matched binding is consumed on `flags.consumed`, which
defaults to true, and only `flags.performable` (default false, not set on
these two) makes an unperformed action fall through to encoding. Key
swallowed on the way in, an action nobody ran, no bytes out, nothing logged.

Windows Terminal is innocent throughout: _KeyHandler matches the user's
action map before offering the key to the engine, and WT has no ctrl+enter
default, so it passed the key down correctly - into ghostty's table.

Unbound in the settings translator rather than patched out of ghostty:
which keybindings exist is the embedder's business, and this is the seam for
saying so. A named key parses to the same physical trigger the default
registers (Binding.zig, Trigger.parse), so these match.

The encoding half is a ghostty patch (input: ctrl+enter is LF). Without it
this change would only have traded silence for `\x1b[27;5;13~`, which the
reporter's applications cannot read either.

Deliberately just these two chords. ghostty's default set holds a dozen more
that WT leaves unbound - ctrl+shift+o/e, ctrl+shift+i, ctrl+shift+q - and
they are dead the same way. `keybind = clear` is the general answer and is a
DESIGN-level call about what the engine owns, so it is written up as a
decision to take rather than taken here on the way past.

Verified on the wire with scripts/probe-enter-keys.ps1 (new): in a ghostty
pane ctrl+enter, ctrl+shift+enter and ctrl+numpad-enter now deliver <LF>,
shift+enter still delivers <ESC>[27;2;13~, plain enter <CR>.

unitControl: Total=76, Passed=76, Failed=0.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01FBNu9ApwBYHifBT6v94fDB
---
 .../GhosttySettingsTranslator.cpp             | 43 +++++++++++++++++++
 .../GhosttySettingsTests.cpp                  | 21 +++++++++
 2 files changed, 64 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttySettingsTranslator.cpp b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttySettingsTranslator.cpp
index 6f14b3507..abae2caf7 100644
--- a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttySettingsTranslator.cpp
+++ b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttySettingsTranslator.cpp
@@ -46,6 +46,49 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
             entries.emplace_back(std::string{ key }, std::move(value));
         };
 
+        // --- Keybindings --------------------------------------------------
+        // Windows Terminal owns the keybindings in this fork: TermControl
+        // matches the user's action map before the key is offered to the
+        // engine at all (_KeyHandler, ahead of _TrySendKeyEvent). Everything
+        // WT leaves unbound is meant to reach the application in the pane.
+        //
+        // ghostty disagrees, because it ships a full application's worth of
+        // its own keybindings and they are still live inside a pane. Two of
+        // them eat a key outright:
+        //
+        //   ctrl+enter       -> toggle_fullscreen
+        //   ctrl+shift+enter -> toggle_split_zoom
+        //
+        // Reported from use: ctrl+enter did nothing in a ghostty pane while it
+        // worked in a cascadia one - measured with a byte dumper across all
+        // three keyboard modes, and *nothing* arrived in any of them, which is
+        // what pointed at a binding rather than at an encoding.
+        //
+        // Declining the action does not hand the key back, and that is the
+        // part worth remembering. GhosttyControlCore reports both of these
+        // unhandled - WT owns window chrome and pane zoom - and DESIGN.md
+        // notes that returning false for NEW_TAB/NEW_SPLIT is "correct, not a
+        // gap". It is not sufficient, though: in Surface.maybeHandleBinding a
+        // matched binding is consumed on `flags.consumed`, which defaults to
+        // true, and only `flags.performable` (default false, and not set on
+        // these two) makes an unperformed action fall through to encoding. So
+        // the key was swallowed on the way in, an action nobody ran, and no
+        // bytes out.
+        //
+        // Unbound here rather than patched out of ghostty: which keybindings
+        // exist is the embedder's business, and this is the seam for saying so.
+        // A named key parses to the same physical trigger the default
+        // registers (Binding.zig, Trigger.parse), so these match.
+        //
+        // Deliberately just these two. ghostty's default set holds a dozen more
+        // that WT leaves unbound - ctrl+shift+o/e (new_split), ctrl+shift+i
+        // (inspector), ctrl+shift+q (quit) - and they are dead the same way.
+        // Clearing the whole set is the obvious general answer and is a
+        // DESIGN-level call about what the engine owns, so it is written up as
+        // a decision to take rather than taken here on the way past.
+        set("keybind", "ctrl+enter=unbind");
+        set("keybind", "ctrl+shift+enter=unbind");
+
         // --- Font ---------------------------------------------------------
         // WT's fontFace is a CSS-style list, not one name: "Cascadia Code,
         // Segoe UI Emoji" means the first family for text and the second for
diff --git a/src/cascadia/UnitTests_Control/GhosttySettingsTests.cpp b/src/cascadia/UnitTests_Control/GhosttySettingsTests.cpp
index cf3700f77..0dc3ddb25 100644
--- a/src/cascadia/UnitTests_Control/GhosttySettingsTests.cpp
+++ b/src/cascadia/UnitTests_Control/GhosttySettingsTests.cpp
@@ -47,6 +47,7 @@ namespace ControlUnitTests
         TEST_METHOD(CursorShapeIsForwarded);
         TEST_METHOD(IntenseTextStyleIsForwarded);
         TEST_METHOD(BackslashesAreDoubledForTheWordCharsFieldOnly);
+        TEST_METHOD(GhosttysOwnEnterKeybindingsAreUnbound);
         TEST_METHOD(NullSettingsProduceNothing);
 
     private:
@@ -429,4 +430,24 @@ namespace ControlUnitTests
         const Control::IControlSettings none{ nullptr };
         VERIFY_ARE_EQUAL(0u, Control::implementation::TranslateGhosttySettings(none).size());
     }
+
+    // ghostty ships its own keybindings and they are live inside a WT pane.
+    // ctrl+enter is bound to toggle_fullscreen there, and a bound key is
+    // consumed before it is ever encoded - so ctrl+enter produced no bytes at
+    // all in a ghostty pane, in every keyboard mode, while a cascadia pane sent
+    // LF. Declining the action is not enough to get the key back: `consumed`
+    // defaults to true and only `performable` (which these two bindings do not
+    // set) falls through.
+    //
+    // Pinned because the symptom is pure silence and the cause is in a
+    // different repository. An upstream rebase that re-adds a binding on a key
+    // WT does not bind will look exactly like this again.
+    void GhosttySettingsTests::GhosttysOwnEnterKeybindingsAreUnbound()
+    {
+        const auto entries = Control::implementation::TranslateGhosttySettings(*_settings());
+
+        VERIFY_IS_TRUE(_has(entries, "keybind", "ctrl+enter=unbind"));
+        VERIFY_IS_TRUE(_has(entries, "keybind", "ctrl+shift+enter=unbind"));
+    }
+
 }
